1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,630
Every piece of a building is designed to

2
00:00:02,670 --> 00:00:03,910
carry a number.

3
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:04,840
A beam.

4
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:05,910
A bolt.

5
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:07,160
A weld.

6
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:09,510
Each one has a load it's rated for,

7
00:00:09,550 --> 00:00:11,950
with a margin on top, in case the world

8
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:14,520
turns out heavier than anyone expected.

9
00:00:15,308 --> 00:00:18,468
On a Friday evening in July 1981,

10
00:00:18,508 --> 00:00:20,628
in a hotel in Kansas City,

11
00:00:20,668 --> 00:00:23,348
one small steel connection was holding up

12
00:00:23,388 --> 00:00:25,801
two walkways full of people.

13
00:00:25,841 --> 00:00:27,548
It had never been strong enough to meet

14
00:00:27,588 --> 00:00:28,868
the building code.

15
00:00:28,908 --> 00:00:31,508
And months before, a change on a drawing

16
00:00:31,548 --> 00:00:33,558
had doubled what it was being asked to

17
00:00:33,788 --> 00:00:34,598
carry.

18
00:00:35,308 --> 00:00:37,108
At about five past seven,

19
00:00:37,148 --> 00:00:38,918
it reached its limit.

20
00:00:46,058 --> 00:00:48,658
The Hyatt Regency Kansas City opened in

21
00:00:48,698 --> 00:00:50,578
July 1980.

22
00:00:50,618 --> 00:00:53,138
Its centrepiece was a vast atrium,

23
00:00:53,178 --> 00:00:56,445
a glass-roofed lobby several storeys high.

24
00:00:56,485 --> 00:00:58,338
And crossing it, in mid-air,

25
00:00:58,378 --> 00:01:01,005
were three suspended walkways.

26
00:01:01,045 --> 00:01:03,058
The second-floor walkway hung directly

27
00:01:03,098 --> 00:01:05,298
beneath the one on the fourth floor.

28
00:01:05,338 --> 00:01:07,698
The third-floor walkway ran on its own,

29
00:01:07,738 --> 00:01:09,588
off to one side.

30
00:01:10,308 --> 00:01:12,988
The walkways didn't rest on columns.

31
00:01:13,028 --> 00:01:15,388
They hung from the roof on slender steel

32
00:01:15,428 --> 00:01:17,918
rods, so they seemed to float across the

33
00:01:18,148 --> 00:01:18,668
room.

34
00:01:18,708 --> 00:01:20,268
People loved them.

35
00:01:20,308 --> 00:01:22,108
From up there, you could look straight

36
00:01:22,148 --> 00:01:24,478
down onto the lobby floor.

37
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:27,276
On Friday evenings that summer,

38
00:01:27,316 --> 00:01:30,343
the hotel held a tea dance in the atrium.

39
00:01:30,383 --> 00:01:31,943
On July the seventeenth,

40
00:01:31,983 --> 00:01:34,783
around sixteen hundred people came.

41
00:01:34,823 --> 00:01:36,983
Dozens of them stood on the walkways,

42
00:01:37,023 --> 00:01:39,353
watching the dancers below.

43
00:01:42,642 --> 00:01:44,762
To understand what happened next,

44
00:01:44,802 --> 00:01:48,012
you only need to understand one joint.

45
00:01:48,725 --> 00:01:51,165
Each walkway was carried by cross-beams

46
00:01:51,205 --> 00:01:53,005
called box beams.

47
00:01:53,045 --> 00:01:55,725
Each box beam was made from two C-shaped

48
00:01:55,765 --> 00:01:58,605
steel channels, welded edge to edge to

49
00:01:58,645 --> 00:02:00,525
form a hollow box.

50
00:02:00,565 --> 00:02:02,845
A steel rod passed through a hole in that

51
00:02:02,885 --> 00:02:03,885
box.

52
00:02:03,925 --> 00:02:06,445
Underneath, a nut and a washer held the

53
00:02:06,485 --> 00:02:07,645
beam up.

54
00:02:07,685 --> 00:02:09,885
All the weight of that section of walkway

55
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:12,735
pressed down onto that one nut.

56
00:02:13,433 --> 00:02:15,873
The original design was simple.

57
00:02:15,913 --> 00:02:18,513
One long, continuous rod would run from

58
00:02:18,553 --> 00:02:20,273
the roof, through the fourth-floor

59
00:02:20,313 --> 00:02:22,433
walkway, and straight on down to the

60
00:02:22,473 --> 00:02:24,273
second-floor walkway.

61
00:02:24,313 --> 00:02:27,193
Each nut carried only one walkway.

62
00:02:27,233 --> 00:02:29,393
The rod itself carried both,

63
00:02:29,433 --> 00:02:32,163
and steel rods are very good at that.

64
00:02:32,892 --> 00:02:35,572
But even this design had a problem.

65
00:02:35,612 --> 00:02:38,052
When federal investigators later analysed

66
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:40,292
it, they concluded that the connection

67
00:02:40,332 --> 00:02:42,052
would have reached only about sixty

68
00:02:42,092 --> 00:02:44,612
percent of the capacity the Kansas City

69
00:02:44,652 --> 00:02:45,542
building code

70
00:02:46,012 --> 00:02:46,772
expected.

71
00:02:46,812 --> 00:02:48,372
Before anything changed,

72
00:02:48,412 --> 00:02:50,902
it was already under-rated.

73
00:02:54,183 --> 00:02:56,703
A single continuous rod has a practical

74
00:02:56,743 --> 00:02:57,743
problem.

75
00:02:57,783 --> 00:03:00,143
It's long, and the nut for the fourth

76
00:03:00,183 --> 00:03:01,983
floor has to sit somewhere in the middle

77
00:03:02,023 --> 00:03:02,943
of it.

78
00:03:02,983 --> 00:03:05,103
Getting it there, and building the walkway

79
00:03:05,143 --> 00:03:07,713
around it, is awkward work.

80
00:03:08,433 --> 00:03:11,073
So during construction, the detail was

81
00:03:11,113 --> 00:03:12,033
changed.

82
00:03:12,073 --> 00:03:14,633
Instead of one rod, there would be two.

83
00:03:14,673 --> 00:03:16,873
The first ran from the roof down to the

84
00:03:16,913 --> 00:03:18,820
fourth-floor box beam.

85
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:21,673
A second, separate rod hung from that same

86
00:03:21,713 --> 00:03:23,593
box beam, down to the second-floor

87
00:03:23,633 --> 00:03:24,793
walkway.

88
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:27,353
On paper, the two arrangements look almost

89
00:03:27,393 --> 00:03:28,843
identical.

90
00:03:29,558 --> 00:03:31,278
But follow the load.

91
00:03:31,318 --> 00:03:33,758
The second-floor walkway no longer hangs

92
00:03:33,798 --> 00:03:34,798
from the roof.

93
00:03:34,838 --> 00:03:37,358
It hangs from the fourth-floor walkway.

94
00:03:37,398 --> 00:03:39,998
So the nut under the fourth-floor box beam

95
00:03:40,038 --> 00:03:41,918
now carries the fourth floor,

96
00:03:41,958 --> 00:03:44,448
and everything hanging beneath it.

97
00:03:45,142 --> 00:03:47,582
Picture two climbers on a rope.

98
00:03:47,622 --> 00:03:49,182
Each one holds the rope,

99
00:03:49,222 --> 00:03:51,982
and each grip carries one person.

100
00:03:52,022 --> 00:03:54,169
Now the lower climber lets go,

101
00:03:54,209 --> 00:03:56,222
and holds on to the upper climber's ankles

102
00:03:56,262 --> 00:03:57,502
instead.

103
00:03:57,542 --> 00:03:59,742
Nothing about the rope has changed.

104
00:03:59,782 --> 00:04:01,822
But the upper climber's grip is now

105
00:04:01,862 --> 00:04:03,632
holding two people.

106
00:04:04,350 --> 00:04:06,817
That was the fourth-floor connection.

107
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:09,390
A joint that was already under-rated was

108
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:12,040
now being asked to carry roughly twice the

109
00:04:12,310 --> 00:04:13,160
load.

110
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:17,170
So how did a change like that get through?

111
00:04:17,210 --> 00:04:19,860
The steel fabricator proposed the two-rod

112
00:04:19,970 --> 00:04:22,610
detail and prepared the shop drawings.

113
00:04:22,650 --> 00:04:24,610
Those drawings went back to the structural

114
00:04:24,650 --> 00:04:27,140
engineers, and returned with their review

115
00:04:27,410 --> 00:04:27,970
stamp.

116
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:29,570
What was said along the way,

117
00:04:29,610 --> 00:04:31,730
including a disputed phone call,

118
00:04:31,770 --> 00:04:33,490
became one of the most argued-over

119
00:04:33,530 --> 00:04:36,210
questions in engineering ethics.

120
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,650
What the investigations made clear is that

121
00:04:38,690 --> 00:04:40,690
the connection was never checked against

122
00:04:40,730 --> 00:04:42,580
the doubled load.

123
00:04:43,308 --> 00:04:45,668
And there had been a warning sign.

124
00:04:45,708 --> 00:04:47,908
While the hotel was still being built,

125
00:04:47,948 --> 00:04:50,668
part of the atrium roof collapsed.

126
00:04:50,708 --> 00:04:52,068
Nobody was killed.

127
00:04:52,108 --> 00:04:53,748
The engineers investigated,

128
00:04:53,788 --> 00:04:55,708
and assured the owner of the atrium's

129
00:04:55,748 --> 00:04:56,708
safety.

130
00:04:56,748 --> 00:04:58,988
The flaw in the walkway connections was

131
00:04:59,028 --> 00:05:00,678
not caught.

132
00:05:03,975 --> 00:05:06,095
Friday, July the seventeenth,

133
00:05:06,135 --> 00:05:08,015
1981.

134
00:05:08,055 --> 00:05:09,935
The atrium is full.

135
00:05:09,975 --> 00:05:11,855
There's music, and there are people on the

136
00:05:11,895 --> 00:05:14,655
floor, and on the walkways above.

137
00:05:14,695 --> 00:05:16,815
The fourth-floor walkway is carrying its

138
00:05:16,855 --> 00:05:19,335
own crowd, and the entire second-floor

139
00:05:19,375 --> 00:05:22,465
walkway below it, with its crowd too.

140
00:05:23,183 --> 00:05:25,623
At about five past seven in the evening,

141
00:05:25,663 --> 00:05:27,543
at the fourth-floor box beams,

142
00:05:27,583 --> 00:05:29,863
the welded seams split.

143
00:05:29,903 --> 00:05:31,903
The nuts and washers pulled straight

144
00:05:31,943 --> 00:05:33,303
through the steel.

145
00:05:33,343 --> 00:05:35,583
The fourth-floor walkway dropped onto the

146
00:05:35,623 --> 00:05:38,023
second-floor walkway, and both fell onto

147
00:05:38,063 --> 00:05:40,393
the crowded lobby below.

148
00:05:45,625 --> 00:05:48,385
One hundred and fourteen people died.

149
00:05:48,425 --> 00:05:50,945
Two hundred and sixteen were injured.

150
00:05:50,985 --> 00:05:53,265
Rescuers worked through the night to reach

151
00:05:53,305 --> 00:05:55,345
those trapped beneath the wreckage.

152
00:05:55,385 --> 00:05:57,425
It was the deadliest structural collapse

153
00:05:57,465 --> 00:06:01,155
in American history, until 2001.

154
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:06,553
The National Bureau of Standards,

155
00:06:06,593 --> 00:06:09,033
known today as NIST, led the federal

156
00:06:09,073 --> 00:06:10,553
investigation.

157
00:06:10,593 --> 00:06:12,713
Its engineers recovered the hanger rods

158
00:06:12,753 --> 00:06:15,483
and box beams, and load-tested matching

159
00:06:15,553 --> 00:06:17,593
connections in the laboratory.

160
00:06:17,633 --> 00:06:19,673
Their conclusion was stark.

161
00:06:19,713 --> 00:06:21,473
The load on the connection at the moment

162
00:06:21,513 --> 00:06:24,113
of collapse was only about thirty-one

163
00:06:24,153 --> 00:06:25,993
percent of what a connection designed to

164
00:06:26,033 --> 00:06:28,313
the Kansas City code should have been able

165
00:06:28,353 --> 00:06:29,803
to hold.

166
00:06:30,517 --> 00:06:32,877
In other words, this was not a structure

167
00:06:32,917 --> 00:06:35,917
pushed past its rating by a freak event.

168
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:38,317
Its rating had been quietly cut,

169
00:06:38,357 --> 00:06:41,407
long before the crowd ever arrived.

170
00:06:44,725 --> 00:06:47,725
So who decided, and who paid?

171
00:06:47,765 --> 00:06:49,965
The change made one detail simpler to

172
00:06:50,005 --> 00:06:51,965
fabricate and install.

173
00:06:52,005 --> 00:06:54,285
That was the entire upside.

174
00:06:54,325 --> 00:06:56,765
The downside was a wave of lawsuits from

175
00:06:56,805 --> 00:06:58,925
victims and their families.

176
00:06:58,965 --> 00:07:01,525
Missouri's licensing authorities found the

177
00:07:01,565 --> 00:07:03,485
engineer of record and the project

178
00:07:03,525 --> 00:07:05,805
engineer grossly negligent.

179
00:07:05,845 --> 00:07:08,085
Both lost their engineering licences in

180
00:07:08,125 --> 00:07:10,255
Missouri, and their firm lost its

181
00:07:10,325 --> 00:07:12,125
certificate to practise engineering in the

182
00:07:12,165 --> 00:07:13,455
state.

183
00:07:14,183 --> 00:07:16,623
Engineering ethics courses still debate

184
00:07:16,663 --> 00:07:18,543
how the responsibility should have been

185
00:07:18,583 --> 00:07:19,583
shared.

186
00:07:19,623 --> 00:07:21,583
But the lesson the profession took was

187
00:07:21,623 --> 00:07:22,543
blunt.

188
00:07:22,583 --> 00:07:26,033
If your stamp is on it, it's yours.

189
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:32,113
After Kansas City, the industry tightened

190
00:07:32,153 --> 00:07:34,353
how connection designs and shop drawings

191
00:07:34,393 --> 00:07:35,843
are reviewed.

192
00:07:35,913 --> 00:07:38,513
Today, a change like that two-rod detail

193
00:07:38,553 --> 00:07:40,033
is supposed to trigger a fresh

194
00:07:40,073 --> 00:07:42,913
calculation, not just a stamp.

195
00:07:42,953 --> 00:07:45,353
The Hyatt Regency is now taught in

196
00:07:45,393 --> 00:07:47,353
engineering ethics classes across the

197
00:07:47,393 --> 00:07:48,780
United States.

198
00:07:48,820 --> 00:07:50,993
Not because the physics is complicated.

199
00:07:51,033 --> 00:07:53,473
It's one rod, two nuts, and a load that

200
00:07:53,513 --> 00:07:54,433
doubled.

201
00:07:54,473 --> 00:07:56,673
It's taught because the number was always

202
00:07:56,713 --> 00:07:58,483
there to be checked.

203
00:07:59,183 --> 00:08:01,383
Every structure around you was built to

204
00:08:01,423 --> 00:08:02,823
carry a number.

205
00:08:02,863 --> 00:08:04,983
Most of the time, there's a margin on top

206
00:08:05,023 --> 00:08:07,063
of it that you'll never see.

207
00:08:07,103 --> 00:08:09,543
The question is always the same.

208
00:08:09,583 --> 00:08:13,193
How far past the rating can it really go?

209
00:08:16,267 --> 00:08:18,227
If you want the next investigation,

210
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:19,347
subscribe.

211
00:08:19,387 --> 00:08:21,557
There's a new one every week.

